<script module>
  export { demoPostExcerpt, noscript, how, intro, hue, spread, roleModel, wheel, shades, lightness, chroma, fade, saturation, gamut, desaturation, selection, oklch, module, creator, credits }
</script>

{#snippet noscript()}

Color Horse needs JavaScript to generate palettes. Please turn it on to use the app.

{/snippet}

{#snippet intro()}

Color Horse generates a harmonious color scheme from any two colors. It uses modern color science to select complementary colors that maintain a sense of perceptual uniformity.

{/snippet}

{#snippet how()}

Color Horse uses a color space called "OKLCH" to generate palettes. OKLCH emphasizes _perceptual uniformity_ between different color shades. That means you can choose a red and a blue (or any two colors) that have a similar feeling of richness or lightness.

To begin, Color Horse extracts the hue from each of your colors. These two hues can be placed on a circle.

{/snippet}

{#snippet hue()}

In basic color palettes, the hues have a uniform spacing around the color wheel. In the real world, this is a very rare pattern. Instead of spacing the colors evenly, Color Horse creates a smooth gradual distribution, so some colors are slightly closer together and some are slightly farther apart. You can see how the spacing changes as you change the hues in your two input colors.

{/snippet}

{#snippet spread()}

Spread sets how many hues sit between your two colors. By default, your secondary color takes the place on the wheel nearest its own hue. At a spread of 2, your colors are neighbors, and the other ten hues fill the rest of the wheel. At 12, all ten sit between them. The wheel stretches and squeezes to fit, and no hue ever passes another.

{/snippet}

{#snippet roleModel()}

This palette was made with an earlier version of Color Horse, so its roles stay as they were. Update to keep Success, Info, Warning, and Danger in their own color families.

{/snippet}

{#snippet wheel()}

By default, Color Horse spaces hues around the RGB color wheel that screens use. Switch to the RYB wheel, the red, yellow, and blue wheel painters use, to give more room to reds, oranges, and yellows and to pair complements the traditional way: red with green, yellow with violet. RYB palettes come out warmer and more old-fashioned. The RYB wheel comes from [RYBitten](https://github.com/meodai/RYBitten) by David Aerne.

{/snippet}

{#snippet shades()}

Next, Color Horse extracts the lightness from each of your input colors. This gives us two starting points to create a gradient of shades from light to dark. Color palettes typically use different shades for different elements — backgrounds, buttons, text, headings.


{/snippet}

{#snippet lightness(svg)}

Color Horse creates eight shades — in addition to the two provided by the two input colors — and places them smoothly around the inputs, resulting in ten shades from light to dark. Set Shades to make anywhere from 4 to 20 instead. You can also set the lightest and darkest shade to increase or reduce the palette's contrast.

{/snippet}

{#snippet chroma()}

After hue and lightness, OKLCH uses one more property to identify each color: saturation, or "chroma." Chroma is just as important as hue in defining a color. Chroma is the difference between brown and red, beige and yellow, grey and blue.

When we place our colors on a circle by hue, we can also represent their saturation as their position on the circle's radius, where the center of the circle is grey (the same for all colors) and the circumference of the circle is a fully-saturated rainbow.

{/snippet}

{#snippet saturation()}

As you'll see below, every shade in our palette has a different chroma. So if your two input colors have different shades, they won't exist in the same "chroma circle." Nonetheless, they are anchor points for the saturation of every other color in the palette. As you adjust their saturation, you'll see the rest of the palette shift.

{/snippet}

{#snippet gamut()}

Screens can show more saturated colors than ink on paper. If your palette will be printed, set the gamut to Print. Color Horse then keeps every color within what standard offset printing on coated paper (FOGRA39) can reproduce, reshaping the whole palette rather than clipping individual colors. Vivid blues, violets, magentas, and greens change the most. If one of your input colors can't be printed, Color Horse uses the closest printable version.

{/snippet}

{#snippet desaturation()}

Color palettes typically lower the saturation of the lightest and darkest colors of the palette. This makes the mid-range colors the most vibrant while the colors used for text and backgrounds are softer.

{/snippet}

{#snippet fade()}

By lowering the saturation of the lightest and darkest shades, we create a curve in the lightness gradient. A stronger curve will create a softer palette. A straighter curve will create a more vibrant palette.

{/snippet}

{#snippet demoPostExcerpt()}

