{/snippet}

{#snippet selection()}

For a website, a simple color palette scheme a few hues for brand colors and a few hues for "semantic" colors that have generic meanings (like red for "danger"). Color Horse automatically chooses these colors for you:

- Primary (your first input) <div class="swatch" style="--color: var(--primary-05);"></div>
- Secondary (your second input) <div class="swatch" style="--color: var(--secondary-05);"></div>
- Tertiary (symmetrical hue of second input) <div class="swatch" style="--color: var(--tertiary-05);"></div>
- Accent (opposite hue of first input) <div class="swatch" style="--color: var(--accent-05);"></div>
- Info (a blue) <div class="swatch" style="--color: var(--info-05);"></div>
- Success (a green) <div class="swatch" style="--color: var(--tip-05);"></div>
- Warning (a yellow or orange) <div class="swatch" style="--color: var(--warning-05);"></div>
- Danger (a red) <div class="swatch" style="--color: var(--danger-05);"></div>

The result is a simple, cohesive color scheme.

The full palette includes four more hues not included in the color scheme. You could use these hues if you like, but for most color schemes eight colors is more than enough.

{/snippet}

{#snippet oklch()}

> [!note]
> ### What is OKLCH?
>
> To learn more about OKLCH, read [Lea Verou's excellent explanation](https://lea.verou.me/blog/2020/04/lch-colors-in-css-what-why-and-how/),

{/snippet}

{#snippet module()}

Color Horse also exists as a zero-dependency npm package called `colorhorse`. Install it to generate color schemes in your own code.

```sh
npm install colorhorse
```

Pass two colors to `getScheme`. It returns each role's shades as hex colors, from darkest to lightest.

```js
import { getScheme } from "colorhorse";

getScheme("#3366cc", "#cc6633");
// { primary: ["#...", ...], secondary: [...], ... }
```

Options match the settings here: `wheel`, `gamut`, `shadeCount`, `minLightness`, `maxLightness`, `minChroma`, `contrast`, and `colorTwoSlot` for spread.

> [!warning]
> ### Bugs ahead
>
> Please note that this package is in beta. Feel free to use it, but please report bugs on GitHub.

{/snippet}

